  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses monosemanticity as a training target and reports how its MonoLoss method performs. It does not test whether superposition or capacity pressure explains naturally occurring polysemanticity.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports results from training SAEs and vision models with MonoLoss specifically to induce more semantically coherent activations. Those results are recorded as findings about induced monosemanticity, not evidence that unmodified models exhibit superposition. The paper cites Toy Models of Superposition but does not test its toy-model claims. Qualitative examples and figure results without separately extractable numerical values are not rows.",
    "test_reason": "The paper introduces MonoLoss, a training objective that induces monosemanticity in vision SAEs and fine-tuned backbones, and reports only its method's gains (MonoScore, class purity, R², accuracy) against baselines. It does not test toy-model superposition, capacity, LLM feature spread, SAE canonicity or feature dimensionality. The behaviour (monosemanticity) is induced on purpose by a loss, so its results do not bear on any version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct. The authors also conclude that their linear-time MonoScore makes in-training use feasible (about 1200× evaluation speedup, about 4% training overhead). They add that MonoLoss as a fine-tuning regularizer gives small accuracy gains, up to 0.6% on ImageNet-1K for ResNet-50. They draw no conclusions about superposition.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains vision SAEs and fine-tunes vision backbones using a loss based on MonoScore; it does not reproduce the toy-model methods in Toy Models of Superposition.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported effects vary by encoder and SAE architecture. The authors note particularly low purity for Vanilla ReLU and describe stronger regularization as reducing or eliminating scores for some latents in certain settings.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Adding MonoLoss to vision SAE training raises ImageNet class purity in all 12 encoder–SAE pairs. The gain is near zero for Vanilla ReLU, and the largest gain is BatchTopK–ViT binary purity, 0.152→0.723.",
        "proposed_claim": "An explicit monosemanticity loss increases class purity of vision SAE latents, with gains depending on SAE architecture and encoder.",
